PYTHON(1)                      P32W Unix                       PYTHON(1)

NAME
       python, micropython - Python interpreter (MicroPython)

SYNOPSIS
       python [script [args]]
       python -c command [args]

DESCRIPTION
       python is MicroPython 1.29, a compact implementation of
       Python 3 (www.micropython.org). It runs a script, a source
       file, with sys.argv holding the script's name and the
       arguments after it. With -c it runs command instead, with
       sys.argv ['-c', args...].

       With no script and no -c: an interactive session when the
       standard input is a terminal, else the standard input is run
       as a script. In the interactive session python prompts with
       ">>> ", or "... " while a statement is incomplete, and
       prints the value of an expression typed alone. After a line
       ending in ":" the next is indented for you; an empty line
       ends the statement: Backspace (which takes back the indent)
       then Enter, or Enter three times. The line editor has the
       arrow keys, ^A ^E ^B ^F ^D ^K ^U and ^W, a history of the
       last 8 lines (up and down arrows, ^P ^N), and Tab to
       complete names and attributes. ^C raises KeyboardInterrupt;
       ^D at an empty prompt, or sys.exit(), leaves; ^E at an empty
       prompt starts paste mode (no indenting; ^D runs what was
       pasted).

       micropython is another name for python.

NOTES
       Integers have no size limit; floats are IEEE doubles, in
       software, correctly rounded (+ - * / sqrt), so they give the
       same results, and print the same, as MicroPython on any
       other machine: repr(0.1) is 0.1, 1e300 * 10 is inf.

       The built-in modules: sys, os (listdir, stat, mkdir, remove,
       rename, chdir, getcwd, getenv, putenv, system, ...), time
       (time, sleep, localtime, gmtime, mktime, ticks_ms, ...),
       math, cmath, json, re, random, struct, collections, errno, io,
       gc, array, heapq, hashlib (sha256), binascii, deflate,
       uctypes, platform and micropython. help('modules') lists
       them. Files are P32W's own, through open(). import looks in
       the current directory, then /usr/lib/micropython.

       MicroPython is not CPython: the standard library is much
       smaller (no subprocess, socket, threading, select or
       asyncio), and some details differ (see "MicroPython
       differences from CPython" at docs.micropython.org).

EXAMPLES
       python -c 'print(2 ** 100, 7 // 2, 1 / 3)'
              1267650600228229401496703205376 3 0.3333333333333333
       echo 'import sys; print(sys.argv)' > a.py; python a.py x y
              ['a.py', 'x', 'y']
       python    then, at the >>> prompt: 1 + 1

FILES
       /usr/lib/micropython
              Python modules for import

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 after an uncaught exception; 2 if the script cannot be
       opened or the options are wrong; sys.exit(n) exits with n.

SEE ALSO
       lua(1), basic(1), sh(1). MicroPython is by Damien P. George
       and others; MIT licence (/usr/src/micropython/README.P32W).

BUGS
       Python objects live in a heap of 2 MiB, fixed when python
       starts (less, down to 256 KiB, when memory is short). A
       Python function calling Python does not use C's stack, so a
       recursion goes about 10,000 calls deep before
       "RuntimeError: pystack exhausted"; one that passes through C
       (a class's __init__ creating another instance, for one) stops
       sooner, about 950 deep, with "maximum recursion depth
       exceeded", when C's stack (1 MiB) runs out.

       MicroPython's own conversion of text to float is not exact
       at the edges of the range: 5e-324 reads as 1.8e-323, and
       some numbers below 1e-307 or near 1.8e308 are a few units
       off in the last digit (MicroPython on any machine does the
       same). The maths functions (sin, atan2, exp, log, ...) are
       correctly rounded for 99.5% or more of their arguments
       (math(3)), so now and then a result differs in its last
       digit from another machine's.

       It is slow: P32W has no floating-point hardware, and the
       compiler does not optimise. Starting python takes about 3 s
       (emulator time), most of it reading the 1.5 MB program from
       the disk; then a Python function call takes about 2 ms and a
       simple loop step 0.35 ms.
